/-!
# Absolute geometry of `Spec ℤ`

The geometries in which `Spec ℤ` has a product with itself, tested against Weil's and Stepanov's
arguments for curves: the arithmetic square `Spec W(ℤ)` in ghost coordinates, its Frobenius graphs,
contacts and gluing, its universal property and `p`-derivations, its Teichmüller points and lattices
of sections, its archimedean fibre, the toric surfaces of a base, the carries, the comparison with
Weil's off-diagonal and with the adelic carrier, and Weil's archimedean form as the finite part of
the jets: the diagonal on combs of degree points, the symbol on the carrier, the form of every
packet on the degree points and on invariant combs, the whole pairing of the degree points as the
pole minus Weil's functional, and the tower of lines that carries the contacts with a count; the
pairing as twice the product of the degrees minus the sum over the zeros, its sign on bounded
apertures and its dependence on the contacts and on the comb; the ring of the tower, the pole
plane and the jets with its section count; the carry against the window count; the remainder of
the zero count on the square and its reading by the contacts at a height; the Hodge index of
the pairing; the carry as a cocycle class; the square of a Dedekind domain; the pairing of the
square of a quadratic ring with its conductor and its Hodge index; the square of a cyclotomic
ring with its contacts on a progression; the constants of the counterterm as the threshold
of the inequality; the planar square, a subring of the square with unit masses in which every
contact is paid; the squares that satisfy Bézout at an aperture as the window cone; the exact
Hodge index of the square of a number ring; Riemann–Hurwitz for the counterterm with the
genus of a degree point; the exchange law between the contacts and the constant of the
counterterm; the tree of a prime glued along the comb, whose edge length the inequality
forces; the length of the tree of `2` as a monotone deformation of the pairing on packets
shorter than `log 4`; the count of a section on two graphs, the split norm; the pole term
as the scale of the contacts; the zeros of a function of order one weighed by two vertical
lines, and Weil's explicit formula for every such function with a functional equation; Weil's form on step packets as the limit of the form on block-constant divisors, its
sign as the Riemann hypothesis, and the inequality at finite scale in both directions; the
path of a contact paid at its price, monotone at every prime; the Hecke `L`-functions of a
number field with their explicit formula, the tempered configuration of their zeros and their
Hodge index, the ideals of a number ring as degree points, and the inequality below the first
contact for a large discriminant; the count of sections in a box as the volume over the gluing
index; the form on the comb in the increments of the divisor; and the Li ladder as the forms
of the Laguerre tails themselves, with the form of every pair of logarithmic tails as a sum
over the zeros; and a ring that is not abelian, the ring of the cube roots of two, with its
degree points and its Hodge index, and the index law of its Artin character of degree two
through the cubic character of two. See `AbsoluteGeometryOfSpecZ/README.md`.
